@* Table's Loading data page, moved from its Overview: rows from a database or an API, paging
   without a total, then a failed load. *@

<Heading Level="2">Data from a database</Heading>
<TextBlock>A table can load its rows from a database, which then sorts, filters and pages them. The simplest way is to pass an EF Core <Code>IQueryable</Code> as <Code>Items</Code>. For more control over the query, set <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> and load the rows yourself.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>The provider receives a <Code>DataRequest</Code> and returns a <Code>DataResult</Code>. The request has these members:</TextBlock>
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem><Code>Skip</Code> and <Code>Take</Code>: the range of rows to load.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Code>Sort</Code>: the sort, first key first.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Code>Filter</Code>: the filter, or null for none.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Code>Cursor</Code>: the cursor of the page to load, when the table pages by cursor (see <Anchor Href="docs/components/table/loading-data#paging-without-a-total">Paging without a total</Anchor>).</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Code>CancellationToken</Code>: canceled when the table no longer needs the answer.</ListItem>
</List>
<TextBlock>The request also carries the table's columns, which set how each key reads a value from an item. <Code>request.ToResultAsync</Code> uses them to count the rows the filter matches and load the page, through EF Core's async methods:</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Provider</Code>
<TextBlock>The filter becomes a LINQ expression on each column's property, so the database does the filtering. A value typed into a filter never becomes code.</TextBlock>
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem>To apply the request without counting, call <Code>request.Apply(query)</Code>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>To apply only the filter or only the sort, call <Code>ApplyFiltering</Code> or <Code>ApplySorting</Code>.</ListItem>
</List>
<TextBlock>To filter a column your own way, such as a value stored differently from how it's shown, set the column's <Code>Filter</Code> to a method that returns the condition:</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@CustomCondition</Code>

<Heading Level="2">Data from an API</Heading>
<TextBlock>When the rows come from an API, the provider passes the request on to it. <Code>request.ToQueryString()</Code> writes the whole request for the URL, such as <Code>sort=-date&amp;skip=20&amp;take=10&amp;filter=total ge 100</Code>. The API reads it back with <Code>DataRequest&lt;Order&gt;.Parse</Code>.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>The API applies the request through the fields it declares, so a request can't reach any other property. <Code>DataFields.Of&lt;T&gt;()</Code> declares every public property. Call it on a type made for the API, or leave a property out with <Code>[ScaffoldColumn(false)]</Code>.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="csharp">@OverTheWire</Code>
<TextBlock>The request sets its own <Code>Take</Code>. If a client shouldn't be able to ask for every row, cap <Code>Take</Code> on the server, as this code does.</TextBlock>
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem>To read one column's condition, call <Code>request.Filter?.Find("status")</Code>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>To call an OData endpoint, write the request with <Code>ToODataQuery()</Code>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>The request also serializes with <Code>System.Text.Json</Code>.</ListItem>
</List>
<TextBlock><Anchor Href="docs/common/data-api">Data from an API</Anchor> has a full client and server, the filter syntax, and OData. <Anchor Href="docs/common/data">Data and filters</Anchor> lists the operators.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Paging without a total</Heading>
<TextBlock>Some data pages by cursor: each page comes with an opaque token for the next one, as many APIs and logs do. Other data has no total. For either, set <Code>Paging</Code> to a paging style that pages by cursor, and return the next cursor from your <Code>ItemsProvider</Code>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Paging style</th><th>What the user sees</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>DataPaging.Pages</Code> (the default)</td><td>Numbered pages and "1–10 of 42"</td><td>Required</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>DataPaging.NextPrevious</Code></td><td>First, Previous and Next, by cursor</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>DataPaging.LoadOnScroll</Code></td><td>The next page added under the rows on scroll (see <Anchor Href="docs/components/table/loading-data#loading-on-scroll">Loading on scroll</Anchor>)</td><td>Optional</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>
<TextBlock>The table passes the cursor back to the provider as <Code>request.Cursor</Code>.</TextBlock>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Cursor</Code>
<TextBlock>If your source has a cursor for the page before, return it as <Code>Previous</Code> too. Without it, interactive tables go back through the cursors they've already followed, and static tables offer First.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-cursor-pages" />

<Heading Level="2">Loading on scroll</Heading>
<TextBlock>With loading on scroll, the next page loads as the last row comes into view, and its rows are added under the ones shown. Set <Code>Paging="DataPaging.LoadOnScroll"</Code>. Give the table a <Code>MaxHeight</Code> too, so the rows scroll in their own box and the content under the table stays within reach.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-load-on-scroll" />
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem>On interactive pages, the rows are added in place. On static pages, the table follows its Load more link, and enhanced navigation adds the rows without a full reload, keeping the scroll position.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>The Load more button under the last row is for keyboard users. It stays out of sight until it has focus. To change its text, set <Code>LoadMoreText</Code>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>With <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">JavaScript off</Anchor>, Load more shows as a link. It reloads the table with one more page of rows: <Code>?page=3</Code> shows the first three pages, loaded from the provider in one request.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>With an <Code>ItemKey</Code>, a row already shown isn't added twice.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>When the parent re-renders, the added pages stay. To start over, call <Code>ReloadAsync()</Code>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Code>NextPrevious</Code> and <Code>LoadOnScroll</Code> also work with <Code>Items</Code>. The table then pages by row offset.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A visible Load more button suits a list rather than a table. For one, use a <Anchor Href="docs/components/list-view">ListView</Anchor>.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">When loading fails</Heading>
<TextBlock>If the <Code>ItemsProvider</Code> throws, or an <Code>IQueryable</Code> in <Code>Items</Code> does, the table logs the exception. In place of the rows, it shows "Couldn't load the rows." with a Retry button. To show your own message, set <Code>ErrorContent</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="table-load-error" />
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem>On static pages and with JavaScript off, Retry is a link to the same URL.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>When loading on scroll, the rows already shown stay, and the message and Retry appear under them.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A request the table canceled because a newer one replaced it isn't an error. Pass <Code>request.CancellationToken</Code> on to stop the work.</ListItem>
</List>

@code {
    private const string Provider = """
        <Table TItem="Order" ItemsProvider="LoadOrders" PageSize="10">...</Table>

        @code {
            async ValueTask<DataResult<Order>> LoadOrders(DataRequest<Order> request)
            {
                var query = db.Orders.AsNoTracking().Where(o => o.Active);
                return await request.ToResultAsync(query,             // filters, sorts, pages
                    count: (q, ct) => q.CountAsync(ct),
                    list: (q, ct) => q.ToListAsync(ct));
            }
        }
        """;

    private const string CustomCondition = """
        <PropertyColumn Property="o => o.Tags" Filterable Filter="TagFilter" />

        @code {
            // Tags are stored lower case, as "red;green": "Green" in the filter finds it.
            static Expression<Func<Order, bool>>? TagFilter(FieldFilter f) =>
                f.Operator == FilterOperator.Contains && f.Value is string tag
                    ? o => (";" + o.Tags + ";").Contains(";" + tag.ToLower() + ";")
                    : null;
        }
        """;

    private const string OverTheWire = """
        // In the browser (WebAssembly): the provider calls the API with the request.
        async ValueTask<DataResult<Order>> LoadOrders(DataRequest<Order> request) =>
            (await http.GetFromJsonAsync<DataResult<Order>>(
                $"api/orders?{request.ToQueryString()}", request.CancellationToken))!;

        // On the server: only the fields declared here can be filtered or sorted.
        app.MapGet("/api/orders", async (HttpContext http, ShopDb db) =>
        {
            var request = DataRequest<Order>.Parse(http.Request.QueryString.Value ?? "")
                with { CancellationToken = http.RequestAborted };
            request = request with { Take = Math.Min(request.Take ?? 100, 100) };   // at most 100 rows
            return await request.ToResultAsync(db.Orders.AsNoTracking(), DataFields.Of<Order>(),
                count: (q, ct) => q.CountAsync(ct), list: (q, ct) => q.ToListAsync(ct));
        });
        """;

    private const string Cursor = """
        <Table TItem="AuditEvent" ItemsProvider="LoadEvents" PageSize="200" Paging="DataPaging.NextPrevious">...</Table>

        @code {
            async ValueTask<DataResult<AuditEvent>> LoadEvents(DataRequest<AuditEvent> request)
            {
                var page = await api.ListEventsAsync(cursor: request.Cursor, limit: request.Take ?? 200);
                return new(page.Items) { Next = page.Next };   // no total needed
            }
        }
        """;
}
